<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Matanza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as 1932 Salvadoran peasant massacre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Matanza, 'the massacre', was the killing of thousands of peasants, most of them indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pipil">Pipil</a>, by the Salvadoran army and militias after an uprising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>-growing west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> in January 1932. Estimates of the dead range from about 10,000 to over 30,000. It entrenched military rule under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximiliano-hernandez-martinez">Maximiliano Hernández Martínez</a> and led indigenous communities to hide their identity for generations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-matanza.jpg" alt="La Matanza" width="320" height="177" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Netito777, vectorizada por Jarke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_levantamiento_campesino_1932.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By 1929 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> made up 75 to 95 percent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>'s exports, and most plantations belonged to the so-called Fourteen Families. When world coffee prices collapsed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>, plantations failed and many workers lost their jobs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farabundo-marti">Farabundo Martí</a>, a leader of the Regional Federation of Salvadoran Workers, founded the Communist Party of El Salvador with Miguel Mármol in 1930. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arturo-araujo">Arturo Araujo</a>, elected in 1931, cut the military budget and was overthrown in a coup on 2 December 1931, after which General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximiliano-hernandez-martinez">Maximiliano Hernández Martínez</a> became acting president.</p><p>In January 1932 the government cancelled the municipal elections the communists had won in the west, and at least thirty communists were killed in Ahuachapán during the voting. A party commission sent to Colonel Joaquín Valdés, the minister of national defence, reached no compromise. On 18 January Martí, Alfonso Luna and Mario Zapata were arrested.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Late on 22 January 1932 thousands of peasants in the west, armed with sticks, machetes and shotguns, rose in revolt. Rebels under Francisco Sánchez took Juayúa, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> merchant Emilio Radaelli and his family were killed, and the rebels also seized Colón, Nahuizalco, Salcoatitán, Sonzacate and Tacuba. Pipil rebels led by Feliciano Ama captured Izalco on 23 January. An estimated 2,000 people were killed by the rebels. The government declared martial law, and troops under Colonel Marcelino Galdámez retook the towns; the rising was crushed by the afternoon of 25 January, with around 100 soldiers killed.</p><p>From 25 January reinforcements under General José Tomás Calderón carried out reprisals in the west, above all against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pipil">Pipil</a>. In several towns the entire male population was gathered in the centre and killed by machine gun fire. The killings went on for about two weeks. Martí, Luna and Zapata were shot by firing squad on 1 February, and Ama was lynched in Izalco on 28 January.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 11 July 1932 the Legislative Assembly passed Directive 121, which granted an unconditional amnesty for crimes committed in putting down the rebellion. Martínez was confirmed as president and ruled until 1944, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> recognised his government on 26 January 1934. Some scholars call the killings an ethnocide, since the army used indigenous dress, appearance and language to choose its targets, and the spoken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pipil">Pipil</a> language was nearly lost. In 1980 left-wing groups took Martí's name for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farabundo-marti-national-liberation-front">Farabundo Martí National Liberation Front</a>, and in 2010 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauricio-funes">Mauricio Funes</a> apologised to the indigenous communities on behalf of the state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Matanza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In January 1932 it crushed a peasant and indigenous uprising in the western coffee districts; the ensuing massacre, known as <b>La Matanza</b>, killed perhaps 10,000 people or more, though estimates vary widely.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Military</a></li></ul></section>
